Z||000006||LarryB||09-03-2001||10:27 PM||||The physical download transfer rate in the US over POTS (dialup) line is capped at 53KBps, upload is 33.6KBps, although the new V.92 spec will support upload speeds of 48KBps.  Anything over 33.6KBps download speed requires there be no analog to digital conversions between the source and your modem.  The theoretical maximum is 64KBps, based on the standard of 8000 ea 8-bit samples per second. <P>Higher effective throughput is possible via compression at both ends, assuming the data being transferred is compressible. <P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B> Good point.  I forgot to capitalize my B in my KBps.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Isn't there a physical limit to the transfer rate over an analog connection (like a dial-up modem)?  I remember reading about all of that when 56k was the new big thing.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||09-04-2001||07:54 PM||||I just did the math for our DSL connection. (Yes, I am bored.)  Our connection is rated at 1.5Mbps down/384Kbps up.  Of course, that in <B>bits (b)</B> so if I figure 8 bits per byte and 1024K per 1MB (mega<B>byte</B>s) i came up with 192KB down, 48KB up. Today I managed to download a file at 204KBps, so apparently they don't really "cap" our speed.  Which is good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Compared to a 56k<B>b</B>ps dial-up, which is limited to 53kb down and 31.2kb (in the US and Canada) according to what I see on the modem ads.  <P>So that would be a max of 6.625KBps down and 3.9KBps up.  That sure does suck.  Like I said before, the highest I ever got on dial-up was 6KBps.  The usual was 5KB.<P>OK, I'm done... back to my school work.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
